    <section id=3D"driver">
      <h2>USB driver</h2>
      <p class=3D"sub">Three native 32-bit DLLs in <code>AppFiles\PlugIns\H=
armonyRemoteDriver_32</code>. They use the standard Windows HID driver, wit=
h no custom kernel driver.</p>
      <div class=3D"tbl"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Plugin call</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>getDevices()</code></td><td>Lists connected remotes=
 with token, VID, PID, skin and report lengths.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>addUsbDevice(vid, pid)</code></td><td>Registers a V=
ID/PID pair to watch for.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>getAttributeByKey(key)</code></td><td>Reads a plugi=
n attribute, e.g. <code>plugin_version</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>performOperation(deviceId, packets, numberOfRespons=
es)</code></td><td>Sends HID packets and returns the responses. Every proto=
col step uses this.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cancelCommand(deviceId)</code></td><td>Aborts the r=
unning command.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>cleanupRemote()</code>, <code>cleanupAll()</code></=
td><td>Releases handles.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p><code>SDE.dll</code> holds the remote manager and HID channel clas=
ses. <code>UsbDevice.dll</code> exports <code>OpenChannel</code>, <code>Rea=
d</code>, <code>Write</code>, <code>GetVendorId</code>, <code>GetProductId<=
/code> and the input/output packet sizes, and calls <code>HidD_*</code> plu=
s <code>DeviceIoControl</code>. <span class=3D"chip ok">Verified</span></p>
    </section>

    <section id=3D"molson">
      <h2>Molson protocol</h2>
      <p class=3D"sub">The protocol family the driver's device table assign=
s to <code>046D:C122</code>. It works like a tiny file system over HID.</p>
      <p>The driver's config lists two Molson entries for this PID: model 9=
6 (skins 91=E2=80=9396, "Martini") and model 66 (skins 66=E2=80=9375, "Memb=
er Web"). Both support five operations: <code>identifyremote</code>, <code>=
connectivity</code>, <code>userconfiguration</code>, <code>learnir</code> a=
nd <code>firmwareupgrade</code>. Which model your 600 reports is still to b=
e confirmed.</p>

      <div class=3D"packet">
        <h3>Request packet</h3>
        <div class=3D"bytes" role=3D"img" aria-label=3D"Packet layout: serv=
ice ID, command ID, sequence number, parameter count, then parameters">
          <div class=3D"byte hdr"><span class=3D"off">byte 0</span><span cl=
ass=3D"val">0xFF</span><span class=3D"nm">service ID</span></div>
          <div class=3D"byte hdr"><span class=3D"off">byte 1</span><span cl=
ass=3D"val">cmd</span><span class=3D"nm">command ID</span></div>
          <div class=3D"byte hdr"><span class=3D"off">byte 2</span><span cl=
ass=3D"val">seq</span><span class=3D"nm">sequence no.</span></div>
          <div class=3D"byte hdr"><span class=3D"off">byte 3</span><span cl=
ass=3D"val">n</span><span class=3D"nm">param count</span></div>
          <div class=3D"byte prm"><span class=3D"off">byte 4=E2=80=A6</span=
><span class=3D"val">params</span><span class=3D"nm">byte, word, dword or s=
tring</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class=3D"caption">Example: open the system info file is <code>FF=
 01 00 02 "/sys/sysinfo" "R"</code>. The response echoes <code>FF 01</code>=
, carries the file handle at byte 5 and the file size (big-endian, 4 bytes)=
 at byte 7. Packets are padded to the remote's report length. <span class=
=3D"chip ok">Verified</span></p>
      </div>

      <div class=3D"tbl"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Cmd</th><th>Name</th><th>Used for</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class=3D"num">
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x00</td><td>Ping</td><td>Check the remote=
 responds. With one zero parameter it acts as a USB reset.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x01</td><td>Open</td><td>Open a file: <co=
de>/sys/sysinfo</code>, <code>/ir/ir_cap</code>, user config, firmware.</td=
></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x03</td><td>Write</td><td>Write user conf=
ig or firmware data.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x04</td><td>Read</td><td>Read system prop=
erties or captured IR.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x05</td><td>Flush</td><td>Commit a config=
 or firmware update.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x06</td><td>DevCtrl</td><td>Stop IR learn=
ing, choose the checksum algorithm.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0x07</td><td>Close</td><td>Close a file, l=
eave learning mode.</td></tr>
          <tr><td class=3D"mono">0xFF</td><td>Reset</td><td>Reset the remot=
e. Parameter <code>0x66</code> resets its file system.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class=3D"caption">From the template for model 96, architecture 16,=
 and the other Molson templates. <span class=3D"chip ok">Verified</span></p=
>

      <h3>Template engine</h3>
      <p><code>Web.Driver.dll</code> embeds about 230 XML "ROM" templates c=
overing Logitech remotes from the Harmony 510 to the Ultimate One. Each tem=
plate defines <code>commandbuilder</code> steps: the packets to send, how m=
any responses to expect, values to check, and values to store (for example =
<code>int:BE::file.size</code>). Placeholders like <code>%%remote.request.p=
acket.length%%</code> are filled in at runtime. The same engine also unders=
tands an older plain-HID protocol.</p>
    </section>

